That's because 'ps ax' isn't going to help.  Try using 'ps aux' which
actually shows memory usage.

Also, try typing 'free' before and after.  It will show you how much
memory and swap you are using.

Finally, even if nothing is necessarily leaking, using lots of different
programs can lead to a situation where the memory that has been allocated
later in the user session is in smaller chunks than it has requested
because of having to work around all of the other chunks that are in use.
This is called memory fragmentation and it slows the computer down just
the same way that hard drive fragmentation does.  It is typically a
result of having less system memory than would be optimal for your uses.
The cure is to buy more memory.  There isn't really any way for you to
determine if you have this problem, but your symptoms are a good indicator.

One of the things that can help is using a program that uses so much
memory that it causes your computer to swap, then exits.  This will cause
all of the pages used by background and sleeping programs to be swapped
and will flush out your buffer cache.  Here's the one I use; I have 256MB
please adjust accordingly:

#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

/* to smash 235MB:
gcc memsmash.c -o memsmash -Wall
strip memsmash
memsmash 3000 10000
*/

int main( int argc, char *argv[] )
{
unsigned long **mat;
size_t d1, d2;
int i, j, k;

if( argc < 3 )
        {
        fprintf( stderr, "Usage: memsmash <dim1> <dim2>\n" );
        exit(1);
        }

d1 = (size_t)atol( argv[1] );
d2 = (size_t)atol( argv[2] );

mat= (unsigned long **)malloc( d1 * sizeof(long *) );
mat[0] = (unsigned long *)malloc( (d1*d2) * sizeof(long) );

for (i=1; i<d1; i++)
        mat[i] = mat[i-1]+d2;

for(k=0; k<10; k++)
for(i=0; i<d1; i++)
        for(j=0; j<d2; j++)
                mat[i][j] = (unsigned long)rand()+k;

return 0;
}

Remember when giving this it's arguments that unsigned long on an alpha
is 8 bytes and you should leave about 20MB for the system.

Regards,

Tony Hammitt

