Andrew `Hex' Munkres (amunk@efn.org) wrote:

: On mine, I tried to use MILO's ls command on my / partition (which is
: >2GB) and it failed.

That very well may be.  Check with 'tune2fs -l ...' characteristics of
these partitions.  Even if blocks are 1024 and no sparse superblocks
were used it is possible that your milo is using 'lseek' to read
directory as well and this may have troubles like you encountered.
Remember that you are not dealing with a "real ls" but some very simple
minded built-in file system reader meant to help a tiny bit in a boot
phase.  One would have to check milo sources to know for sure.  Like I
mentioned - milo will definitely keel over a boot image located "too far".

But you claimed in your previous posting that Milo cannot read
partitions over 16 MB.  This is definitely not the case.

: Both / and /boot partitions use ext2fs.

See my earlier posting in this thread.  As far as milo is concerned
e2fsprogs-1.14 and e2fsprogs-1.17 or 1.18 are quite different animals
and due care is required.

  Michal



