Happens to me once in a while, too. I get around it by:

Pressing in the Halt button (middle one, below power)
Power cycling the machine
The machine halts at the triple prompt (>>>)
Type in "b dka400 -fl /kernels/generic.gz -fl "root=/dev/scd0 expert text"

This usually get the install going. If not, try this at teh tripple prompt
instead:

>>>set bootdef_dev dka400
>>>set boot_file /kernels/generic.gz
>>>set bootos_flags root=/dev/scd0 expert text
>>> b

This saves you typing in anything. One thing to remember is that these SRM
parameters MUST be changed once Linux is up and running, or it'll try to
boot from CD forever. So, after the install is done

>>> set bootdef_dev dka0
>>> set boot_file
>>> set boot_osflags 0

BTW, make sure you have the latest BIOS. Another resource is
http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/linux/install2041.pdf - Compaq wrote this
for the DS10 & it's bretheren, but the SRM install is pretty much identical.

Good luck!

Ron Sokoloski

