Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

JJ (johnj@bicnet.net) wrote:
: Unlike Unix, you cannot have a "C" partition which spans the entire disk
: overlapping other partitions.

Just to clear things before anybody will get confused.  Contrary to 
the statement above you **may** have a partiton which spans the entire
disk, although Linux does not **require** it.  You really want to have
such partition if the disk will be used in a dual boot machine running
also DU or DU may get quite unhappy if it sees the disk.  This
partition be better marked 'unused'.

DU is using such partition to get an access to an entire raw device.
With Linux you have things like /dev/sda so such partition is
superfluous but it does not mean that it banned.  You may also take
a disk partitioned under DU, or BSD, change partition types and
lay on them new file systems with mke2fs.

: For linux, DO NOT overlap any partitions..
: else you will corrupt the filesystems..

Creating active writable file systems on overlapping partitions will
lead eventually to a corruption under any OS.  I think that you may
refer to "off-by-one" bug in various versions of fdisk.  When
proposing the last sector of the last partition it likes to give
a number higher by one than a total numbers of sectors on a disk.
Accepting that will "wrap" the last partition making it to overlap
with a boot block and bad things may indeed happen.

  Michal



