> I currently run Linux on x86 (yuch!) hardware but I would like an alpha
> based Linux server. Where can I get a DEC Alpha mobo & chip in Yorkshire
> (UK)? Will this fit into a standard AT case? What mobo & processor should I
> be looking for as I would like to migrate my current EDO RAM (72 pin), IDE
> disks/CDROM & S3 Trio3D gfx to the new system (if at all possible).

I build my own alpha after buying board+cpu+mem from Aspen Systems.
I got a LX board with a 533MHz CPU. (can still perform wonderfully
on floating point code).

For this board a standard ATX case is OK.
You can use standard IDE storage (although at the time I bought it,
linux IDE performance was bad, so SCSI was the only real option).
Standard peripheral stuff like floppy, mouse, kb.
And most PCI cards and ISA cards will work just fine.

Exception to this is a nVidia graphics card like TNT. This card
uses a BIOS with x86 code (which is OK, because the alpha can emulate
x86). But the catch is that it also uses 32 bit x86 code, which the
alpha does not emulate.

I bought the ram (DIMMS) from Aspen also, because they told me Alphas
can be quite picky when it comes to ram.

There are quite a few UK addresses listed at:

http://www.alphalinux.org/hardware/vendors.shtml

PW Navigate has been doing alpha quite some time now, and has a good
support section at their website.

