Andrew `Hex' Munkres (amunk@efn.org) wrote:

: That should work except that the kernel is called vmlinuz, not
: vmlinux.gz.

A kernel is called the way you named it.  As far as Milo and Linux
are concerned you may call it "RedRidingHood" as well.  It does not
matter as long as you are pointing to a correct device and a name.

: (IIUC MILO can't read >16MB partitions, which is why it
: couldn't read hda1.)

Could you, please, get into a habit of checking your information before
you post? This is not the first time.

Milo can read all partitions which Linux kernel can.  After all it is
based on the same sources (which is easy to check).  But, current
versions of milo, or at least those I am aware of, will have troubles
loading a kernel which is - whole or in parts - located further then a
2 GB __offset__ from a partition beginning.  The easiest way to prevent
that from happening is to keep a partion from which milo load a kernel
below that "magic" size.  There are also other good reasons to keep root
and/or boot partitions relatively small and 2 GB is usually way too big
anyway for that purpose.

What is more, if a particular milo is based on kernel sources from a 2.0
series (most of them floating around) then it will not read partitions
with block sizes other then 1024 and file system features like sparse
superblocks.  It is good to keep that in mind when creating a file
system which milo will have to read.  mke2fs from versions 1.17 and 1.18
will use by default sparse superblocks and 4096 block size for file
systems "big enough" unless explicitely told otherwise.  Red Hat
installer is aware of that tidbit.

  Michal



