  You don't need to make an extended partition, try:
fdisk /dev/sdb
  create a new primary partition
  write the disk table and exit
mke2fs /dev/sdb1
  (that stands for MaKe Ext2 File System)
  
  You should then be able to mount and use sdb1.  Edit /etc/fstab to get
it to mount on startup, and you're done.

