"Newton Love" <newtlove@mindspring.com> writes:

> Hi all,
> 
> What's up?: RedHat 6.1 installation
> Hardware: DEC Multia/UDB (universal desktop box)
>   500 MB internal,
>   64 MB RAM with 201 MB disk for swap
>   two Seagate 1 GB dicks for /usr and /home.
> 
> Trouble. MILO install doesn't work.
> 
> The instructions say to make a <10MB unused partition
> on the first scsi drive (as the first partition, so it can have the
> boot block on it, then to make the rest (diskhog) for
> a partition to hold the  / dir.

I think that you may have misunderstood the directions. You need 2 small
partitions, a FAT16 partition for MILO and an ext2fs partition for the
kernel and related files.

> When I used the disk tool (it looked like disk druid), it was a little
> piece of sh*t! No matter what order I told it to make partitions,
> it ALWAYS made the disk hog start on cyclender 1, and made the
> unused partion at the end of the disk.

After playing around with that program for a while, I was able to get it
to put a small partition at the beginning of the disk (although it
didn't help me set up MILO for another reason!) Anyway, Disk Druid
really is bad!

> Finally, after a successful install of RH, I used the ksh console
> in the install to run fdisk, which explicitly put the partions
> in the correct order.
> 
> It didn't work. When the RH install got to where it was going to
> parition the drives, it wouldn't format the paritions.

Try this: Run the install in "expert mode" so you can run fdisk, create
a small FAT16 partition sized at least 1.44MB (for MILO) as partition
sda1, write the changes, then reboot. Then, run the installation in
the normal mode, create the rest of the partitions in Disk Druid, tell
it not to format the one you created in fdisk, and install Linux. Boot
the new Linux installation using the MILO diskette, run mkdosfs on the
first partition, mount it and the MILO diskette, cp /mnt/floppy/*
/mnt/fat (or whereever the FAT16 partition is mounted,) reboot, set ARC
to run linload.exe from the first partition, enter the boot command in
the "OS Load Options" field (or whatever it's called; I think that's it
but I'm not sure,) which is probably something like "boot sda3:vmlinuz
root=/dev/sda5" (no quotes,) and it should work.

> Later, I tried to use bsd a disk label. It didn't work either.

If I understand correctly, ARC can only read DOS-style labels and SRM
can only read BSD-style labels. BTW, I would highly recommend using SRM
(I don't 'cause the manual says only AlphaBIOS works on 164LXs, which my
computer is, but it says that either one works on a Multia.)

> So, my solution was to not have a separate partition. My first
> disk is now all one partition.
> 
> I hate this! In RH 4.x and 5.x, I had a boot blocks, and could just
> turn on the box, and it would load all the way to linux.
> 
> Now, for RH6.x, I have to boot MILO Floppy, then type in the
> command to boot the system from the hard drive.
> 
> Come on guys! I know a lot of UNIX. I did the RH 4 and 5 for myself,
> and had the boot block working. How come I can't get RH 6 to work!
> 
> What changed that I can't figure out?

If I understand correctly, the MILO-ARC/AlphaBIOS method of booting
Linux completely ignores boot blocks and instead runs an executable
binary program file (in this case, linload.exe) from a FAT16
partition. SRM uses boot blocks like x86 boxes do.

