My 2c:

Firstly, sda2 is indeed your extended partition, within which lives
sda5 and sda6, so you can't use it.

Secondly, not so important, but it's probably better to mount
your FAT partition as msdos, not vfat. It'll save you accidently
writing a filename that your ARC doesn't understand (ARC
doesn't do long filenames).

Next, I would probably just use fdisk to create one large partition
sdb1, mke2fs and then mount it as /usr (after copying your
current /usr, of course).

The rest, I'd just leave.

Of course, many people have strong views about where all the
root directories should go, but I'm all for keeping it simple for a
private, home use system.

/Ryan

