Some items of advice:
1)On my 164LX, the CD drive is scd0. (It's a SCSI drive.)
2)I don't know about setting the active device, but that probably
doesn't matter because it's likely that the only command you need to run
in MILO is the boot command. Try this command to boot from the CD:
boot scd0:/kernels/generic.gz root=/dev/scd0
(Replace scd0 with whatever the CD drive is called on your machine.)
If it can't find a kernel there, this is probably because the kernel is
in a different place on the SuSE CD than it is on the RH6.1 CD. Try this
command to find where it might be:
ls scd0:/
(You do know what this command does, correct?)
You could also add "text expert" (no quotes) to the boot command to run
the installation in text mode.

Some problems you may encounter & how to get past them:
1)If one of those commands that read from the CD fails the first time,
just try it again. I don't know why this happened, but it worked for
me. <shrug>
2)If you can't find a kernel on the CD or it won't boot a kernel that is
there, you probably need to write a kernel and/or diskette image from
the CD to (a) diskette(s) and load the kernel and/or fs from those
instead of directly from the CD. (With RH6.1 on my 164LX, the
instructions said to do this, but it didn't work, so I used the boot
command above to run the install program instead!)
