Hello everyone,

I am using the latest ccc with its libcmpla  on a dec alpha lx154 (21164a)
running
RH Linux 6.0 (several kernels from 2.2-5 to 2.2.13.  I also have the latest gcc
2.95
that I can use if it helps solve my problem.


My problem is this: I am doing many,many,many floating point calculations in
generated mathematical formulae
that may trigger floating point exceptions in log,exp,ln,pow,etc. Often these
generated formulae
cause floating point exceptions midway through their evaluation. But I must
continue the evaluation to completion
(I want to simply "skip" the failed function in the formula and passthrough the
prior value).

Here is a contrived example:
    a formula may look like: cos( tan (log(x)))

If tan() fails, then I want cos() to receive the value log(x) (i.e., i want tan
to passthrough the value log(x) to cos().



QUESTION 1:
    What combination of compiler flags, libraries, and code technique (catch the
fp signal?)
    can I use  the meet the following criteria (or close as possible):

        1. allows me to catch a FPE, reset something, then continue on with the
rest of the formula
        (is this even possible? if the fpu triggers a fpe, is it still usable
afterwards?)

        There is some documentation in Chapter 10 of the ccc manual about
catching a FPE that I will
        try soon. Has anyone done this?


and/or

        2. allows me to predict a FPE (domain,range,overflow,underflow) so that
it never occurs so that
        I can reset something, then continue. How exactly do you test for
log,exp,ln,pow,etc ?

        My guess is that you must use -ieee for cccc. Then, can I just check the
results for NaN, Inf, etc?



I prefer #2, and have pursued this somewhat, but I still get FPEs because I have
not found the right
combinations of tests on the function arguments to log,exp,pow,ln,tan, and all
the trig functions.

Also, there is code I am not responsible for that also may trigger the fpe,
which is why catching the
signals is appealing.


Of course, my runs take literally weeks at a time, so a solution that causes a
big performance hit is
not a good option.


QUSTION 2: What is the best combination of compiler flags to get fastest FP
performance using
ccc? gcc?


Thanks a lot for all your replies!
Jeff





