References: <38C994A5.5F52F475@nospam.com> <8a9add$9em$1@nnrp1.deja.com> <mi0ics0bprvoaug4ulhvp4fstm1gj3cju5@4ax.com>
Organization: Technical University Berlin, Germany
Reply-To: bruns@tetibm2.ee.TU-Berlin.DE (Warner Bruns)
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

 Dear G. Hugh Song,

 You wrote in "comp.os.linux.alpha" that you you were experiencing
 that your UP-2000 with two 21264 processors is only a factor of two faster
 than a single K7, when running a finite-difference time-domain calculation.

    I am very familiar with FDTD-computations, I do that myself.
 Since a FDTD-computation needs extremely large memory bandwidths
 ( about 7 bytes of memory per floating point operations ),
 normally the speed of the processor is not the limiting factor, but the speed
 of the memory.

    I do not have experience with UP2000 but I am shure that:
 If you measure the number of floating point operations that your UP2000
 performs on large grids (much larger than the cache), you will see a number
 between 50 and 150 Million floating point operations. This is much less than
 what can be achieved by the processor alone. The reason is simply that the
 memory isn't fast enough.

 If you will measure the performance of your code, I would like to hear the
 numbers.

 Cordially,

 Warner Bruns

