Hello there,

    What follows might sound a little bit off topic, although it should be
interesting for HPC users. I first submitted this bug to the DQS team and
posted a message on comp.parallel.mpi a week ago, ... no reply yet (except
from Rusty, many thanks by the way.) So I am trying to catch more HPC
people' attention onto this.

    We are encountering problems when trying to start parallel jobs with the
DQS batch queuing system (v. 3.2.7). We uses MPICH v. 1.2.0 as our MPI
implementation. Our programs are compiled with Compaq's C compiler (ccc) for
Alpha Linux. At a glance, DQS starts as many processes as requested by the
user, but MPI sees only one of them. Here are more detailed explanations.

    The program to be run in this example is called "truc"; it simply prints
on the screen its process id, the name of the machine on which it runs, and
the number of processes involved in the job (plus a little something to last
long enough, so that we get a chance to see the processes with qstat).
Everything runs fine when starting processes on the local host, or on remote
hosts with mpirun:

deepflow:gallez> mpirun -v -np 2  truc
running /home/kermadec.users/gallez/inma/seance2/truc on 2 LINUX_ALPHA ch_p4
processors
Created /home/kermadec.users/gallez/inma/seance2/PI6963
Process 0 on deepflow.mema.ucl.ac.be, 2 processes running
Process 1 on deepflow.mema.ucl.ac.be, 2 processes running
deepflow:gallez> mpirun -v -np 2 -machinefile cluster -nolocal truc
running /home/kermadec.users/gallez/inma/seance2/truc on 2 LINUX_ALPHA ch_p4
processors
Created /home/kermadec.users/gallez/inma/seance2/PI6865
Process 1 on deepflow01, 2 processes running
Process 0 on deepflow00, 2 processes running

    Things get more complicated when we try and start the processes with
DQS:

deepflow:gallez> cat myscript.sh
#!/bin/csh
#$ -N truc
#$ -cwd
limit coredumpsize 0
truc
deepflow:gallez> qsub -l "ev56.and.(qty.eq.4).and.(-exec.eq.mpirun)"
myscript.sh
your job 4099 has been submitted
deepflow:gallez> qstat -u gallez
gallez   truc      eq_df01         4099   0:4   r       RUNNING   03/03/100
16:42:40
gallez   truc      eq_df05         4099   0:4   r       RUNNING   03/03/100
16:42:40
gallez   truc      eq_df08         4099   0:4   r       RUNNING   03/03/100
16:42:40
gallez   truc      eq_df12         4099   0:4   r       RUNNING   03/03/100
16:42:40
deepflow:gallez> ls truc*
truc*  truc.c  truc.e4099.6774*  truc.hosts4099.6774  truc.o
truc.o4099.6774*
deepflow:gallez> cat truc.hosts4099.6774
deepflow08.mema.ucl.ac.be
deepflow05.mema.ucl.ac.be
deepflow12.mema.ucl.ac.be
deepflow01.mema.ucl.ac.be
deepflow:gallez> cat truc.e4099.6774
deepflow:gallez> cat truc.o4099.6774
Process 0 on deepflow08, 1 processes running
deepflow:gallez>

As you can see, qsub starts 4 processes as requested on four hosts which
meet the ressource request "ev56" (in this case, hosts 08, 05, 12, 01), but
there is only one process defined in MPI_COMM_WORLD, as printed out by
process # 0 (the only process actually doing something).

I modified the arguments to qsub in many ways, by adding "-par mpi" or "-par
generic_all", for example, but nothing really changed.

Is anybody aware of a solution to this ?

Many thanks in advance,

    Xavier

