> The Alpha Architecture specifies both little and big-endian adressing
and
> The Cray T3D and T3E are both big endian Alphas.


My error bothered me for some time, so I went back to the handbook just to
check.

Whilst Alpha is Bi-endian w.r.t. data, but not instructions, I'm sure that
IA64 was going to be fully bi-endian. I seem to recall DEC engineers
lambasting the IA64 design for it's bi-endian character and the amount of
extra chip real estate it was going to take up. Alpha's implementation is
fairly trivial, since it's only about swapping a few bits in the address.

Also, isn't the 'endian-nis' dependent on the surrounding hardware. I.e. all
DEC/Compaq Alphas built to date have been little-endian, with no way
easy/obvious to make them big-endian. The Cray's are obviously purpose built
with special supporting chipsets to enable their big-endian nature, which
Cray wanted for various reasons.

I guess what I'm trying to say is that for practical purposes, while Alpha
as an architecture/chip is bi-endian, there are no freely available Alpha
'systems' that are BI-endian, which might explain why Linux/Alpha is a
little-endian OS. Additionally, even if the types of Alphas that Linux
developers use were bi-endian, in the early stages of the porting process,
I'm sure Windows NT, Digital UNIX and even Linux/Intel were used for
'cross-compiling', which would have been made a little simpler by making
Linux/Alpha little endian.

/Ryan


