I upgraded from 2.2.12 to 2.2.14 and I also put the patch into the
kernel (see previous message for patch details). That does fix the problem,
I can compile with floor() and get correct behaviour (no hang).
I haven't tested all the extreme cases to see that floor() is exactly right
but for all the obvious cases it does the expected thing.

So just to repeat, the patch does work.

I tried a few apps that I had previously compiled and which were hanging
on me and they worked too. Interestingly, the other kludge solution that
involved replacing floor() with round-off is probably FASTER on an EV4
chip than the inline assembly. The reason being that even though the kludge
solution is longer, it doesn't evoke a kernel trap (which is vastly slower).
Then again, the EV4 is pretty slow to begin with, I can understand why libc
would be optimised to work best with the newest machines. Just maybe some
conditional code in the bits/mathinline.h file would be a nice touch so that
it gets the best results all round... maybe something like:

#ifdef __alpha_ev4__

__MATH_INLINE double __floor( double x )
{
	return(( double )( int )(( x < 0 ) ? d1 - nextafter( 1, 0 ) : d1 ));
}
/* correct use of nextafter() ?? */

#else /* __alpha_ev4__ */

/* normal definition here */

#endif /* __alpha_ev4__ */

Anyhow, the difference is probably not worth worrying about (though these
little things add up after a while).

