The Multia doesn't have an AlphaBIOS, it uses the older ARC console.

There is no fdisk/format program in the ARC console, you have to run
one from a FAT-formatted floppy (or CD). For NT, it's ARCINST.EXE,
but I'm sure you can use the Linux installation. I did mine a long time ago,
and changed disks (with both on line for a while), and used fdisk and
mkdosfs to create a small FAT partition, which gets mounted as /milo
on my Multia.

If you're really stuck, I'd recommend pulling the disk out, and installing
it in another system to create the partitions (and copy your MILO on
at the same time). Then you can boot MILO from hard disk and use
the installation floppies to get your installation started.

BTW, MILO needs to be on a FAT partition on a Multia (unless you
Blast MILO into FLASH, but I wouldn't recommend that). Why I hear
people ask ? Because the ARC (and AlphaBIOS) only understands
two file systems: FAT and CDFS (ARC is a Microsoft specification).
It doesn't use the 'boot block' of the disk like an Intel system, so you
can't install MILO in the same way you install LILO on a disk. Some
see this as a problem, I think it's better for several reasons which are
irrelevant to this discussion.

/Ryan
Happy Multia owner

