> The ALPHA can be set to be either, but when running LINUX is LITTLEENDIAN.


Not true IIRC. Alpha is Little Endian, always, same as VAX was, and several
other
DEC architectures before it ( Tru64 UNIX, VMS and Windows NT are all little
endian). The cost of a bi-endian chip is prohibitive if you have no reason
to put it in.

Perhaps you are thinking of IA64, which is (was?) going to have bi-endian
support
for the sake of HP-UX (or more correctly, PA-RISC - big endian) and for
MS-Windows (IA32 - little endian).





